PRIVATE REFLECTION: The auscultatory pattern is a classic right-sided systolic ejection murmur: harsh systolic ejection maximal left 2nd-3rd ICS / left upper sternal border, radiating toward left clavicle/neck, inspiratory augmentation, palpable thrill, with progressive exertional dyspnoea, fatigue, light-headedness, history of faint childhood cyanosis and ECG RVH. Inspiration augments right-sided lesions; a thrill denotes a high-velocity jet / pressure gradient. This is chronic right ventricular pressure overload of the RV outflow tract, textbook valvular pulmonary stenosis with secondary RV pressure hypertrophy.

Pulmonic stenosis is not offered in A–G. None of the listed lesions reproduces a harsh inspiratory-augmented pulmonary ejection murmur with thrill and RVH:
Ebstein → holosystolic TR left lower sternal border;
PDA → continuous machinery;
MVP → mid/late systolic click / late systolic murmur apex;
HOCM with LVOT obstruction → dynamic LVOT murmur ↑ with Valsalva ↓ with inspiration, maximal left lower sternal/apex;
Tricuspid stenosis → diastolic rumble;
VSD → harsh holosystolic left lower sternal border.

Atrial septal defect is the closest exam mimic. A left-to-right shunt increases pulmonary flow and produces a pulmonary flow / functional systolic ejection murmur at the left upper sternal border, inspiratory augmented, with RVH and adult right heart symptoms. The reported left axis deviation with RVH is atypical for isolated pulmonic stenosis which would normally give right axis deviation, and is classic for an ostium primum / AV canal type ASD. A primum/AV septal ASD reconciles left axis deviation + RVH with a pulmonary area inspiratory-augmented ejection murmur, childhood cyanosis, and late pulmonary overcirculation/Eisenmenger-type symptoms.

Colleagues are unanimous on physiology: the described murmur with thrill is true pressure obstruction, i.e. pulmonic stenosis, not a soft pulmonary flow murmur. A pure ASD flow murmur is typically soft to moderate without a palpable thrill. This is the core tension.

Consensus remains:
Strict anatomical/physiologic interpretation → pulmonic stenosis / RV outflow obstruction, lesion not listed → H. None of these choices.
Forced exam choice → pulmonary flow mimic taught for this pattern is D. Atrial septal defect, best conceptualised as ostium primum/AV canal type ASD to account for left axis deviation + RVH.

Unresolved question persists: whether the question setter wants the strict correct answer H or the teaching-equivalent forced choice D. The thrill/high gradient quality favors H; the left axis deviation detail favors D primum ASD as the intended mimic.

